      "A": "Research a sequential data-layout candidate: replace repeated linear ID scans and repeated string tokenization on traversed paths with an ID-to-first-index lookup and compact, input-order adjacency representation (eager or lazy, based on measured crossover). Preserve the observed successor rule exactly: inspect neighbors in token order, ignore unresolved IDs, update only on strict improvement over an initial float zero, and retain the earlier neighbor on ties. Preserve query token spelling, query order, `%f` formatting, and float behavior.",
      "B": "Research a memoized functional-DAG candidate. Once the same immediate-value successor is identified, compute each node’s greedy-chain sum once and reuse it for repeated or converging requested paths; compare lazy reachable-only evaluation with full precomputation. Use float storage and child-before-parent addition order so rounding matches the recursive implementation, and use an iterative chain/state design if it avoids deep recursion. Do not substitute a maximum-total-path algorithm for the source’s greedy immediate-neighbor choice.",
      "C": "Research end-to-end crossover and parallelism for the 4-core ARM target. The valid baseline is only 0.011935 s, so quantify setup/thread overhead and retain a strong serial path; do not force OpenMP. If beneficial, evaluate immutable successor construction or independent completed-chain evaluation in parallel while preserving deterministic ordered output and race-free caching. Also assess low-overhead parsing/allocation/output buffering, but only retain general structural thresholds rather than test-specific special cases.",
      "G": "Conservatively integrate only compatible candidate ideas. Validate every candidate and each actual combination against the unchanged correctness oracle, including ordering, equal/negative neighbor values, repeated queries, and float-output behavior. Benchmark complete integrations under the fixed environment; do not assume A+B+C gains add, since preprocessing, cache footprint, and thread overhead may regress the 11.935 ms baseline. Select the fastest measured correct program, preserving correctness for every valid input and making no compiler, budget, target, permission, test, or oracle changes.",
      "analysis": "The measured baseline is correct on 4 cases and averages 0.011934743 s. The main algorithmic waste is likely recomputing greedy paths while repeatedly doing O(n) ID lookup plus `strcpy`/`strtok`/`atoi` parsing. The source behavior is more specific than a generic maximum path: it follows the direct neighbor with the greatest eligible raw value, then recursively sums that chain. Because fixed startup and I/O costs are substantial at this runtime, representation and memoization improvements need measured crossover decisions, and parallelism is a risk rather than an assumed win."
